-- title:
--   Stack-machine split of an integer item by a ruler 2^m (quotient and remainder)
-- statement:
--   Let $q,i,j,k,t_1,t_2$ be pairwise distinct stacks of a state $\sigma$. Assume stack $i$ is $\mathrm{enc}(z)$ followed by $R_i$ and stack $j$ is $\mathrm{enc}(2^m)$ followed by $R_j$, where $R_i$ and the tops of $k,t_1,t_2$ do not start with a bit symbol. Then `splitR q i j k t1 t2` runs from $\sigma$ to the state where
--   $$i := \mathrm{enc}\big(\operatorname{sg}(z,\lfloor |z|/2^m\rfloor)\big)\mathbin{+\!\!+}R_i,\qquad k \mathrel{+}= \mathrm{enc}\big(\operatorname{sg}(z,\ |z| \bmod 2^m)\big),$$
--   with cost at most $\mathrm{splitCost}(m)+17 = O(m)$. Here $\operatorname{sg}(z,n)$ is $n$ with the sign of $z$, so this is the signed (truncating) division of $z$ by $2^m$; the ruler on $j$ is kept.
--
--   Splitting is used to cut inputs into $M$-bit pieces, to reduce modulo $2^n+1$ (via $2^n\equiv -1$), and to extract carry digits; again the cost depends only on $m$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Machine states are `St S` records: stacks `stk : Fin S → List Sym` over the 16-letter alphabet `Sym = Fin 16` (head = top), input position `ip` and output `out`; `σ.set i L` replaces stack `i`. An integer item is stored as `(Item.int z).enc`: a sign header followed by the minimal binary magnitude, least significant bit nearest the header; `bitOf` detects bit symbols, and the hypotheses `bitOf (…).head? = none` say that the following stack content does not start with a bit (so item boundaries are recognisable). `Runs I prog p σ σ' c` means `p` runs from `σ` to exactly `σ'` with some cost $c'\le c$; the procedures `prog` and input `I` are arbitrary.
-- source:
--   A. Schönhage, V. Strassen, Schnelle Multiplikation großer Zahlen, Computing 7 (1971), 281–292 (main theorem: O(n log n log log n) multitape TM multiplication). Lemma `NLogN.Runs.splitR` of the Lean development NLogN/Shift.lean (lines 230–323), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

-- Schönhage–Strassen multitape-TM development (NLogN), generated by skeleton subtraction.

import Definitions.Def_IntMul_SS_Defs2

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false
set_option maxHeartbeats 1000000

open NLogN
variable {S P : ℕ} {I : List Sym} {prog : Fin P → Stmt S P}

theorem NLogN.Runs.splitR {q i j k t1 t2 : Fin S} (hqi : q ≠ i) (hqj : q ≠ j) (hqk : q ≠ k)
    (hqt1 : q ≠ t1) (hqt2 : q ≠ t2) (hij : i ≠ j) (hik : i ≠ k) (hit1 : i ≠ t1) (hit2 : i ≠ t2)
    (hjk : j ≠ k) (hjt1 : j ≠ t1) (hjt2 : j ≠ t2) (hkt1 : k ≠ t1) (hkt2 : k ≠ t2)
    (ht12 : t1 ≠ t2) (σ : St S) (z : ℤ) (m : ℕ) (Ri Rj : List Sym)
    (hi : σ.stk i = (Item.int z).enc ++ Ri) (hj : σ.stk j = (Item.int ((2 : ℤ) ^ m)).enc ++ Rj)
    (hRi : bitOf Ri.head? = none) (hk : bitOf (σ.stk k).head? = none)
    (ht1 : bitOf (σ.stk t1).head? = none) (ht2 : bitOf (σ.stk t2).head? = none) :
    NLogN.Runs I prog (NLogN.splitR q i j k t1 t2 : Stmt S P) σ
      ((σ.set i ((Item.int (sg z (z.natAbs / 2 ^ m))).enc ++ Ri)).set k
        ((Item.int (sg z (z.natAbs % 2 ^ m))).enc ++ σ.stk k)) (splitCost m + 17) := by sorry
